        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T11:56:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 769,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 706,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120617,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 184,
        "headingId": 706,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>The neoplastic cells reside in the bone marrow.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T11:56:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 770,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 707,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120618,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 184,
        "headingId": 707,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>IgM MGUS is not associated with clinical manifestations. It is usually diagnosed incidentally on laboratory tests performed during workup for a wide variety of clinical symptoms and reasons. IgM MGUS carries a risk of progression to Waldenstr&ouml;m macroglobulinaemia (lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma), although rarely patients can develop IgM plasma cell (multiple) myeloma, chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, or other lymphomas {24222331; 29342381}. It may progress to immunoglobulin light chain amyloidosis. The most important causally related disorders that occur in a small proportion of patients with IgM MGUS are IgM-related peripheral neuropathy and Schnitzler syndrome, a rare disorder characterized by chronic urticaria {25439696}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T11:56:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 771,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 708,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120619,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 184,
        "headingId": 708,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>IgM MGUS is a subset of MGUS, accounting for about 20% of all MGUS cases. Thus, the estimated prevalence in the general population aged &ge;&nbsp;50&nbsp;years is approximately 0.6% {16571879}. The incidence of IgM MGUS increases with age. The age-adjusted incidence is higher in males than females, and higher in Black people than White people. First-degree relatives may have a higher risk of MGUS {19182202}.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T11:56:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 772,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 709,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 120620,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 184,
        "headingId": 709,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>See <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/63/185\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Non-IgM monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance</em> .</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-12T11:56:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 773,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
